As you can see, the model "fits" but we are getting NAs for all the estimates.

My questions are:

1) Any idea why all the estimates are NA?

2) Is SECR the correct way to do this, or would linearsecr be more effective given our study region/design. I have started working with linearsecr, but thought I would introduce you to my current state of work first.

3) If SECR is correct, and the mask is not the issue, are there detection functions or other limiting parameters, that can be used to help inform the model given the dataset. I've read the vignettes, and have spent a bit of time looking in the literature; and messing with the model using 'model = list(g0 = ~1, sigma = ~1), etc.

4) If SECR is correct, would it be better to make a mask using a shapefile of the 5 detectors with circular polygons around each detector, and a mask/buffer to limit the coastline and shallow water nature of the dolphins?
--

I will probably have more questions, but any insight into these thoughts would be greatly appreciated. I'm hoping the data is not too sparse, or that the study design will inherently fail without a serious amount of constraints. We understand that the results may not be significant or informative, but we are looking to show that dolphin signature whistles are an acceptable identifier for sparse, coastal populations.

Let me know if you would like me to provide any more information, data, or code.

Many thanks,
Jack

