I'm glad that helped. Clearing up a couple of points -
1. My use of a hazard-based detection function (parameters lambda0, sigma; codes HHN, HEX etc., rather than HN, EX etc.) was arbitrary - just my habit. I don't think it relates to your comment about density over area. Unfortunately 'lambda' appears in different roles in the literature - as the expected number of animals detected, and as the hazard of detection.
2. I don't know about 'correct'. The mask delimits the places where a dolphin home range might plausibly be centred. Ordinarily we say you can make the mask larger without affecting density estimates, but you have the problem that along-shore movements can be large (>20km). Therefore the 2-D density estimate you get will depend on how wide you make the mask (1 km, 5 km, 10 km); each width _within the movement range of dolphins_ will expand the area from which a detected dolphin might have come. I expect that's fairly opaque! Another reason to think of the habitat as linear.
3. I don't remember where I got lambda0 = 38; any largish initial value will do (lambda0 = 5 and lambda0 =100 give same answer).
Murray

