> > > I am just starting to fit SECR models to a set of data for my PhD, focusing on the endangered Indian Ocean humpback dolphin in southern Africa. We think there are fewer than 500 remaining in the whole country - and given their low density and challenging habitat to work in (surf zone of an exposed coastline, rarely moving into water more than 15m deep) - we have been developing a unique approach to mark-recapture using 'signature whistles' (individually unique whistles) as the 'mark' captured through moored hydrophones. Realistically - there are in the region of 10s of animals in this study area during this study period, certainly not 100s.
> > >
> > > Our study design is slightly unorthodox as we have deployed only 5 hydrophones along a ~120 km portion of the south coast range of this population (see map). We know the dolphins move through and between these bays (from previous photo-ID), as well as beyond them in both directions.  This is meant to be a test case for what we hope will become an effective long-term monitoring strategy.
> > >
> > > We have 5 detectors (SoundTrap hydrophones), with 2 pairs of detectors placed in two of the bays, ~ 2 km apart (signals are not picked up on both, these are treated as independent detectors, not aSCR), and a 5th in between the 2 pairs.
> > >
> > > The survey design was limited based on logistical constraints (number of hydrophones for now). Our deployment length was 5 weeks, and our goal is to estimate the continuous density of our "count" detectors (using the 'model = list(D = ~1)' function, during 1 occasion, I believe).
> > >
> > > I made masks using a the trap file and a polygon of the south coast range shape file (~900 km).
> > >
> > > Mask 1 (low res):
> > >
> > > sousamask_mask <- make.mask(traps("SP_Trap.txt"), spacing = NULL, nx = 100, ny = 10, c("polygon"), poly = home_range_trsfm,
> > >                             buffer = 100)
> > >
> > > Mask 2 (high res):
> > >
> > > sousamask_mask1 <- make.mask(traps("SP_Trap.txt"), spacing = NULL, nx = 1000, ny = 100, c("polygon"), poly = home_range_trsfm,
> > >                             buffer = 100)
> > >
> > >
> > > Here is the summary() output of our CaptureHistory:
> > > > DolphinCH4<-read.capthist("SP_Capt.txt", "SP_Trap.txt", detector = "count")
> > > No errors found :-)
